<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    Entitlements for the direct download (Developer ID and the hardened runtime).
    Kept to the minimum .NET needs: more permissive flags make Apple's notary
    service analyse the app more deeply, and notarisation takes longer.

    The .NET runtime cannot start without these two. The release signs the app
    with the hardened runtime, and CoreCLR's JIT writes and runs generated code in
    W^X pages, which the hardened runtime blocks unless these are granted. Without
    them the kernel kills the process as soon as the JIT runs (the app never
    opens; launchd reports error 163).

    MacWebAuth's ObjC thunks depend on this too: the ASWebAuthenticationSession
    completion block's invoke pointer comes from
    Marshal.GetFunctionPointerForDelegate, which is JIT-generated code.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Vinyl recording opens the audio input (Services/Audio/OpenAlCapture.cs). Under the hardened
    runtime that is refused without this entitlement, whatever the user answers. The usage string
    that goes with it, NSMicrophoneUsageDescription, is written into Info.plist by the release.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    Not in this file, on purpose: the keychain. The app's own signature also
    carries com.apple.application-identifier, com.apple.developer.team-identifier
    and keychain-access-groups (LBW775Z8JG.com.rpm.player), which is where the
    sign-in session is kept. The release adds them to a copy of this file, next to
    the embedded provisioning profile that authorises them, and signs the app with
    that copy after the deep signing pass has signed the bundle with this one.
    They cannot be in this file: the deep pass puts this file on every executable
    in the bundle, and ffmpeg, fpcalc or UpdateMac carrying a restricted
    entitlement without a profile of their own would be killed when they start.

    Never put a double hyphen in these comments: Apple's entitlement parser (AMFI)
    rejects the file, although plutil accepts it.
  -->

  <!--
    Also not in this file, on purpose: com.apple.security.app-sandbox. This file is
    for the direct download. A Mac App Store build needs its own sandboxed
    entitlements, with com.apple.application-identifier and
    com.apple.developer.team-identifier matching an embedded provisioning profile,
    and com.apple.security.network.client. Without that last one the sandbox
    blocks every socket and DNS lookup (getaddrinfo fails with "nodename nor
    servname provided"), which an App Store reviewer sees at the sign-in screen.

    ASWebAuthenticationSession needs no entitlement; the sign-in sheet works under
    both identities.
  -->
</dict>
</plist>
